description:
pkix-truststore/src/lib.rs:437-440 carries a compile-time assertion:

  const _: fn() = || {
      fn _assert_send_sync<T: Send + Sync>() {}
      _assert_send_sync::<Error>();
  };

with a comment noting that TrustAnchor is covered by an equivalent
assertion in pkix-path. This is the right pattern — it ensures the
properties survive future refactors. But it is not exposed in the
rustdoc, so a caller reading docs.rs cannot see the guarantee.

What goes wrong for a caller in production:

  - The natural use pattern is 'load anchors at startup on the main
    thread, share them across worker threads.' Without a documented
    Send+Sync contract, callers either:
      (i)  read the source to verify, which is a cost the maintainer
           could amortize once by writing the docstring, or
      (ii) defensively wrap anchors in Arc<Mutex<...>>, sacrificing
           lock-free read concurrency, or
      (iii) assume Send+Sync without checking, exposing themselves to
           a future maintainer change that drops the property without
           noticing the source-level assertion.
  - Error: Send + Sync matters when the error crosses thread boundaries
    in a worker pool that handles trust-store loads asynchronously. Same
    'read source vs document' tradeoff.

This is a one-line rustdoc fix. Examples in other workspace crates already
do this (e.g., pkix-path has Send+Sync mentioned in TrustAnchor's rustdoc
or workspace stability section — verify and match the existing pattern).

Suggested top-level rustdoc addition to lib.rs after the # Stability
section:

  //! # Thread safety
  //!
  //! `TrustAnchor`, `Error`, and `IoFailure` are all `Send + Sync`.
  //! Trust anchors are typically loaded once at startup and shared by
  //! reference across worker threads or behind `Arc<Vec<TrustAnchor>>`.
  //! Compile-time assertions (see source) enforce this; the property
  //! is part of the public contract.
